{"id":"9cd33f92-4aa8-4fbf-a27b-f1d06962c89d","object":"chat.completion","created":1784801946,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    # Validate case structure\\n    required_fields = ['items', 'required_failure_items', 'unresolved_without']\\n    for field in required_fields:\\n        if field not in case:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Missing required field: {field}\\\")\\n    items = case['items']\\n    if not isinstance(items, list) or not all(isinstance(i, str) for i in items):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"'items' must be a list of strings\\\")\\n    req_fail = case['required_failure_items']\\n    if not isinstance(req_fail, list) or not all(isinstance(i, str) for i in req_fail):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"'required_failure_items' must be a list of strings\\\")\\n    unresolved_without = case['unresolved_without']\\n    if not isinstance(unresolved_without, list) or not all(isinstance(i, str) for i in unresolved_without):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"'unresolved_without' must be a list of strings\\\")\\n\\n    # Define oracle\\n    def test(subset):\\n        subset_set = set(subset)\\n        for u in unresolved_without:\\n            if u not in subset_set:\\n                return 'UNRESOLVED'\\n        for r in req_fail:\\n            if r not in subset_set:\\n                return 'PASS'\\n        return 'FAIL'\\n\\n    # Full set\\n    full = list(items)\\n    if test(full) != 'FAIL':\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Initial full configuration must produce FAIL\\\")\\n\\n    # ddmin algorithm\\n    current = list(items)  # ordered list\\n    n = 2\\n    test_count = 0\\n\\n    while True:\\n        # Build ordered list of items in current (preserve original order)\\n        current_items = [item for item in items if item in set(current)]\\n        m = len(current_items)\\n        if n > m:\\n            n = m\\n        if n <= 1:\\n            break\\n\\n        # Partition into n subsets (contiguous chunks, as equal as possible)\\n        size = (m + n - 1) // n\\n        subsets = []\\n        for i in range(0, m, size):\\n            chunk = current_items[i:i+size]\\n            if chunk:\\n                subsets.append(chunk)\\n        # Ensure exactly n subsets (if last chunk empty? not possible with size>0)\\n\\n        reduced = False\\n        # Test each subset\\n        for subset in subsets:\\n            test_count += 1\\n            res = test(subset)\\n            if res == 'FAIL':\\n                current = subset\\n                n = 2\\n                reduced = True\\n                break\\n\\n        if reduced:\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Test complements\\n        for subset in subsets:\\n            complement = [item for item in current_items if item not in set(subset)]\\n            if not complement:\\n                continue\\n            test_count += 1\\n            res = test(complement)\\n            if res == 'FAIL':\\n                current = complement\\n                n = 2\\n                reduced = True\\n                break\\n\\n        if reduced:\\n            continue\\n\\n        # No reduction: increase granularity\\n        if n >= m:\\n            break\\n        n = min(n * 2, m)\\n\\n    # Ensure 1-minimal (final set must be FAIL, no proper subset fails)\\n    if test(current) != 'FAIL':\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Algorithm produced non-failing set\\\")\\n\\n    # Return result in deterministic order (original ordering)\\n    result_set = [item for item in items if item in set(current)]\\n    return {\\n        \\\"failure_set\\\": result_set,\\n        \\\"test_count\\\": test_count,\\n        \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"FAIL\\\"\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to produce Python code for `solve(case)` that implements ddmin with unresolved semantics. The task is to implement a deterministic delta debugging algorithm (ddmin) that finds a 1-minimal failure-inducing subset (ordered configuration? Actually it says \"minimize a failure-inducing ordered configuration\" but typical ddmin works on subsets. The case has items as list of strings, required_failure_items, and unresolved_without. The oracle: we can test subsets? The problem says \"external test execution is replaced by a deterministic oracle fixture\". So we need to simulate an oracle that given a subset of items (configuration) returns PASS, FAIL, or UNRESOLVED based on the case's fields. The logic: The initial configuration (all items) must produce FAIL. Required_failure_items are items that *must* be present for the failure? Actually typical ddmin: we have a failure-inducing set. Here they give required_failure_items: items that are necessary to cause failure? And unresolved_without: items without which the test is unresolved? We need to interpret.\n\nThe fixture shows cases: items list, required_failure_items, unresolved_without. For case 0: required_failure_items: [\"d2\",\"d3\"], unresolved_without: []. So probably the oracle: A subset S causes FAIL if and only if it contains all required_failure_items? And causes UNRESOLVED if it lacks any of the unresolved_without items? But case 0 has unresolved_without empty, so never unresolved. For case 1: required_failure: [\"d3\",\"d4\"], unresolved_without: [\"d0\"]. So if S does not contain d0, the test is UNRESOLVED. If it contains d0 but does not contain both d3 and d4, then PASS? Need to define.\n\nWe must implement `solve(case)` that returns a minimal failing subset (1-minimal) with the ddmin algorithm. The algorithm: start with full set (all items). Granularity n = 2? Actually typical ddmin: try subsets of size len/2, each complement tested only if subset fails? There's a standard algorithm: ddmin(c) - returns minimal failure-inducing set. Steps:\n1. Let granularity = 2.\n2. Let current configuration = full set.\n3. While granularity <= len(current):\n   - Partition current into roughly equal subsets (size floor(len/granularity) or something).\n   - For each subset, test it. If FAIL, set current = subset, granularity = 2, continue.\n   - If all subsets PASS or UNRESOLVED, then test complements? Actually ddmin tests subsets first; if any fails, reduce to that subset and reset granularity. If all subsets pass or unresolved, then test complements (i.e., the whole minus subset) for each subset. If a complement fails, reduce to that complement and reset granularity.\n   - If no subset or complement fails, then we are done? Actually algorithm: double granularity? Wait standard ddmin: \"If any test passes, increase granularity.\" But here: \"Only FAIL permits reduction; PASS and UNRESOLVED do not.\" \"Decrease granularity after reduction and otherwise double it up to current size.\" So the algorithm: start with granularity 2. Test subsets at current granularity. If any subset FAIL, reduce to that subset and reset granularity to 2. If no subset fails (all PASS or UNRESOLVED), then test complements. If any complement FAIL, reduce to that complement and reset granularity. If no subset or complement fails, then double granularity, unless granularity already >= current size? Then we are done, return current configuration.\n\nAlso: \"1-minimal failure\" meaning no proper subset of the result fails.\n\nWe need to implement oracle. Based on case payload: items list, required_failure_items, unresolved_without. For a given subset S (list of items):\n- If any item in unresolved_without is not in S, return \"UNRESOLVED\".\n- Else if all items in required_failure_items are in S, return \"FAIL\".\n- Else return \"PASS\".\n\nBecause required_failure_items are the items that must be present to cause failure. Unresolved_without: items without which the test is unresolved (cannot determine pass/fail). So if any missing unresolved_without item, test is unresolved. Otherwise, if all required_failure_items present, test fails, else passes.\n\nCheck case 0: required_failure